{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent` is a high-level ownership statement. It says TBV owns four things used by the ask builder: the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission. Refining requirements cover the details: `.isolation`, `.provider_failure`, `submission.*`, `fixed_tools` and others. Those need no separate test here. I mapped each of the four owned areas to the marked tests.\n\n- **Model conversation.**\n  - `supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport` runs a real local HTTP transport. It asserts the request model, token limit and tool list. It also asserts that the first turn carries no `function_call_output` and the second turn carries the delivered tool result (\"a source snapshot\"). That shows tool results go back into the conversation.\n  - `supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat` checks the same for the native Ollama chat path. It asserts the POST `/api/chat` path, the bearer key, options, and a `tool` role message on turn 2.\n  - `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` asserts the exact tool results delivered each turn across four turns.\n  - `concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads` and `agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces` check that concurrent conversations stay separate. The `.isolation` requirement refines that obligation.\n\n- **Supported backend integrations.**\n  - OpenAI and Ollama are exercised end to end against local servers.\n  - Bedrock cannot run without AWS network services. It is covered at unit level in `providers.rs`:\n    - `bedrock_native_option_types_are_checked_and_only_max_tokens_is_defaulted` asserts the defaulted max tokens of 16,384 and rejection of mistyped options.\n    - `cache_checkpoints_are_requested_for_caching_models` asserts which models get cache checkpoints and that only the cache option changes.\n    - `typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history` asserts that typed client tool calls keep their order and native history, and that normalization is idempotent.\n  - I treated the missing Bedrock transport test as an exemption: the shown tests set up no AWS service, and the Bedrock-specific logic is covered by these three tests.\n\n- **Tool dispatch.**\n  - `application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call` shows application tools are built fresh per call and dispatched with results delivered.\n  - `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` shows graph tools (`outgoing_edges`, `read_node`, `get_next_page`) and application tools dispatched together. It also asserts the dependency observations they record.\n  - `source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads` shows source tools dispatched, with observations recorded only for delivered reads.\n\n- **Structured submission.**\n  - The OpenAI and Ollama tests assert the `submit_result` schema (required `[\"result\"]`, boolean type) and the \"Finish by calling submit_result\" prompt.\n  - The answer is asserted as `true` in those tests.\n  - `behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer` asserts the typed structured result and the schema type of its `adequate` field.\n\nI found no plausible violation of the requirement text that these tests would accept. The finer submission, isolation and provider-failure obligations belong to their own requirements.",
  "references": "8ed00f348732b47fae1c4f4a3cd306ae69762b9f4bb6f22913ad71ae1d0c3b86",
